<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pontifical Swiss Guard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1506">1506</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Swiss Guard</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pontifical Swiss Guard is the small armed corps of Swiss soldiers that protects the pope and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apostolic-palace">Apostolic Palace</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> in 1506. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> on 6 May 1527 most of the guard was killed covering the escape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-santangelo">Castel Sant'Angelo</a>, and recruits are still sworn in on that date. It is the last survivor of the Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pontifical-swiss-guard.jpg" alt="Pontifical Swiss Guard" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Versaldi · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BANDERA_GUARDIA_VATICA_LEOXIV.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Guard has its origins in the 15th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> allied with the Swiss Confederacy and built barracks in Via Pellegrino, foreseeing the recruiting of Swiss mercenaries. Innocent VIII renewed the pact to use Swiss troops against the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and Alexander VI used them in his alliance with the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> the Swiss were a fixture on the front lines among the warring factions. Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Giuliano della Rovere</a>, who had been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> and knew the Swiss well, became Pope Julius II in 1503 and asked the Swiss Diet for a constant corps of 200 mercenaries, financed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> merchants Ulrich and Jacob Fugger.</p><p>The first contingent of 150 soldiers left on foot in September 1505 under Kaspar von Silenen and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 22 January 1506, now regarded as the official date of the Guard's foundation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its most significant hostile engagement came on 6 May 1527 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a>. As the troops of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> advanced, 147 of the 189 Guards, including their commander Caspar Röist, died fighting so that Clement VII could escape through the Passetto di Borgo, escorted by the other 42. Clement replaced the depleted Guard with 200 German mercenaries, and in 1537 Paul III ordered it reinstated; anti-papal sentiment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> hindered recruitment.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> the Guard ceased to serve as a combat unit and became mostly a protection and honour guard, though twelve members served at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in 1571. It was disbanded and reinstated in 1800, 1814 and 1849, and in the later 19th century it declined into a ceremonial body with low standards. The modern Guard is the product of reforms by its commander Jules Repond from 1910 to 1921, who recruited only native Swiss, introduced rigorous exercises and designed the Renaissance-style uniform, completed in May 1914. His strictness led to a week of open mutiny in July 1913.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Guard protects the Pope and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apostolic-palace">Apostolic Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a> and accompanies the Pope on journeys abroad. Overall security in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> is the work of a separate body, the Corps of Gendarmerie of Vatican City. After the attempt on the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> on 13 May 1981 the Guard placed greater emphasis on its non-ceremonial roles and equipped itself with modern small arms, though its dress uniform of striped red, dark blue and yellow, high plumed helmet and halberd remain. Recruits must be unmarried Swiss Catholic men between 19 and 30 who have completed basic training with the Swiss Armed Forces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swiss Guard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thereafter Swiss soldiers fought chiefly as mercenaries, above all for France, and from 1506 as the papal <b>Swiss Guard</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Pope Julius II, who founded the <b>Swiss Guard</b> in 1506, personally led his army in winter 1511 and entered the captured town of Mirandola through a breach in its walls.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards the federal state ended the old trade in mercenaries: cantonal service contracts were forbidden by the 1848 constitution and foreign recruitment by a federal law of 1859, leaving the <b>Pontifical Swiss Guard</b> in the Vatican as the main survival.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the storming of the Tuileries Palace on the night of 9–10 August 1792, when the people of Paris forced the king to flee to the National Assembly and massacred his <b>Swiss Guard</b>, the crowd danced and sang the Carmagnole to celebrate.</blockquote><a href="/songs/carmagnole">La Carmagnole</a></li><li><blockquote>The pope is an absolute elective monarch, elected by cardinals in conclave, and governs through a commission and a governor; the <b>Swiss Guard</b> protects him.</blockquote><a href="/state/vatican-city">Vatican City</a></li></ul></section>
